an uncommon sight in the city of buenos  aires tractors from across the country  came to argentina's capital to protest  against the government of alberto  fernandez  andrej miles traveled from the province  of santa fe he explained why he and  others like him are at risk  we cannot produce like this all of us  end up renting our farms because  small-scale farmers end up renting their  land to international corporations who  can afford to bear the enormous tax  burden we have  food prices are rising worldwide driven  up by the war in ukraine this has put  argentinian producers on alert farmers  say taxes export taxes drought and  exchange rate controls are hurting their  capacity to produce and fear the  government could increase taxes even  more  thousands of people accompanied the  farmers to the center of the city to  protest against the administration of  alberto fernandez they say they're tired  of the inflation rate it was close to  seven percent last month and of the  difficulties they face every month  they're saying that the government has  no economic plan and the bureaucracy and  the restrictions implemented by the  administration are hurting argentina's  productive sector  last march the government increased  export taxes on soybean flour and oil  seeking to control local prices and  stabilize the price of wheat to what it  was before ukraine's conflict  it also says farmers are overreacting  and there are no plans to increase  export taxes again at least for now  we have an increased export taxes on the  producers so they are marching just in  case they're marching against the  project they don't know or imagine that  could happen so we have no doubt this is  a political demonstration that has to do  with other interests than defending the  rights of the farmers  journalists like mattia longoni say the  situation inflames already long-running  tensions between the government and the  agricultural sector  the farming sector's in a strange  situation because like most countries in  the world where they produce more food  than they consume there's a chance to  make profit  argentina has a surplus of food and it  could export more grain milk and meat  but in argentina's case the better  prices don't always benefit the farmer  this is the constant trouble between  producers and the government  and that's why farmers vow to continue  with their demands  they say government assistance would  help them to produce more something they  say would benefit argentina's economy  and the whole world  perishable al jazeera buenos aires
 

